Abstract
The last decades have been witness of an ever-growing interests for the synthesis and application of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).  However, most of the current synthetic procedures are limited to yield MOFs in powder state. In this work, hybrid foams were fabricated by using in situ synthesis of leaf-like zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-L) into nanocellulose at room temperature using water as solvent, followed by a gelatin matrix incorporation and freeze-drying. The foams are ultralight weight and are highly porous with densities ranging from 19.18 to $37.4\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ . The shapeability, hierarchical porosity, and low density of the formed foams offer promising potential for applications such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption. The dispersion of  ZIF-L into the cellulose network increases the material accessibility and may open new venues for further MOFs processing.
Keywords: Nanocellulose; Foams; Metal-Organic Frameworks; Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks; $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.
## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), are porous materials consisting of metal and organic moieties (Yao et al., 2015; Yuan et al., 2018; Zou et al., 2016), which have impacted the progress of several applications including $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Chambers et al., 2015; Diercks et al., 2018), $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and conversion (Abdelhamid & Zou, 2018; Ding & Jiang, 2018), catalysis (Emam et al., 2018; Zhang et al., 2018), adsorption (Abdel-Magied et al., 2019; Li et al., 2018), sensing\biosensing (Abdelhamid et al., 2017; Yang et al., 2016), drug delivery (Abdelhamid & Wu, 2019), energy-related applications (Abdelhamid, 2018; Wang et al., 2018), and mass spectrometry (Abdelhamid, 2019). MOFs have unique properties such as large surface area, high porosity, tunable structure and multifunctional applications (Abdelhamid et al., 2019; Falcaro et al., 2016; Gaillac et al., 2017). MOFs are typically synthesized in the form of powders, which hampers their applications, therefore constructing materials such as monoliths and foams have gained increasing interest (Sultan et al., 2018; Wu et al., 2017). However, it remains challenging to synthesize an easy-handling MOFs-based materials with hierarchical porosities for industrial applications and commercial purposes (Abdelhamid, 2017).
Biopolymers, such as nanocellulose and gelatin, are  promising porous supporting materials because they are abundant, produced from renewable sources, biodegradability and inexpensive. Nanocellulose offers great versatility of surface functionalization due to their abundant surface functional groups such as hydroxyl, carbonyl, carboxylic groups. It shows also outstanding mechanical properties. Nanocellulose-based foams are promising candidates for several applications (Lavoine & Bergström, 2017) including heat insulating materials (De France et al., 2017), supercapacitors (Yang et al., 2015), as absorbents (Wei et al., 2017), and flexible devices (Toivonen et al., 2015). Furthermore, nanocellulose provides a universal substrate for the synthesis of nanoparticles such as gold nanoparticles (Eisa et al., 2018), silver nanoparticles (Han et al., 2016), silica (Demilecamps et al., 2015), magnesium hydroxide nanoparticles (Han et al., 2015), graphene oxide (Wan & Li, 2016), and  hybrid carbon nanomaterials (Zheng et al.,  2015). However, very few literature examples were published for combining MOFs and nanocellulose. MOFs such as zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-90) (Matsumoto & Kitaoka, 2016), ZIF8 nanocrystals (Su et al., 2018), Universiteteti Oslo-66 (UiO-66), and Materials of Institute Lavoisier-100 (MIL-100(Fe)) (Zhu et al., 2016), Hong Kong University of Science and Technology-1 (HKUST-1) and ZIF-67 ( Zhu et al., 2018), were reported using cellulose as substrate. However, the current synthetic procedures require most of the times unfriendly solvents (e.g. dimethylformamide, DMF) (Matsumoto & Kitaoka, 2016), high temperature ( $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ )(Su et al., 2018),  multisteps (H. Zhu et al., 2016), and long reaction times (Zhu et al., 2018).
Herein, we reported a one-pot synthesis of gelatin/nanocellulose-leaf-like zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-L) foams, (ZIF-L refers to $\mathrm{Zn(mim)}_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{Hmim)}_{1/2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{3/2},$ , and Hmim refers to 2-methylimidazole). 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl radical (TEMPO)-oxidized cellulose nanofiber (TOCNF) was used as model for nanocelluose. In situ growth of ZIF-L into TOCNF was achieved at room temperature using water as solvent. The formed gel was incorporated into a gelatin matrix, and subjected to freezedrying to obtain ultralight weight foams with good mechanical stability. Applications of the synthesized foams for selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption is also reported. The formed foams offer hierarchical porous structure and is believed to enhance the diffusion of molecules for faster kinetics of adsorption and regeneration, as well as facilitate the scalability and easy processing of adsorbent materials, leading to a reduction in costs.
## Experimental section
## Chemicals and Materials
2-methylimidazole (Hmim), triethylamine (TEA), $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ 2,2,6,6- tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl radical (TEMPO), sodium bromide (NaBr), sodium chlorite (NaClO) and gelatin were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Germany). Deionized water was used for the preparation of all solutions.
## Fabrication of TOCNF
TOCNF was obtained from a defibrillation process of soft wood pulp, following the reported procedures (Isogai et al., 2011). In brief, an aqueous suspension of pulp from Norwegian spruce was subjected to TEMPO-mediated an oxidation steps using TEMPO, NaClO and NaBr. The obtained carboxylated fibers $(1.5\mathrm{mol\cdotg^{-1}})$ ) were subjected to mechanical disintegration using a high-pressure homogenizer to yield fully defibrillated cellulose nanofibrils (CNF).
## Fabrication of hybrid foams
The in-situ growth of ZIF-L into TOCNF networks was carried out at room temperature. First, TOCNF $(15~\mathrm{g},0.5~\mathrm{wt.}\%)$ was added into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker. Secondly, a zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\left(146.4\mathrm{mg},0.49\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ was added into the TOCNF dispersion. The dispersion was stirred for $2\mathrm{min}$ . TEA ( $78.2\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.8 mmol) was added followed by the addition of 2-methylimidazole (Hmim, 634.6 mg, 7.8 mmol). The molar ratios of Zn:Hmim:TEA is 1:16:1.6. After stirring vigorously for $2\mathrm{min}$ , the
milky mixture was left for 1 hour. ZIF-L $@$ TOCNF sample (denoted as MF-1, MOFFoam) was separated using a filter paper, and washed with deionized water. Finally, gelatin (5 g, 4 wt. $\%$ ) was added and the aqueous suspension were mixed using a highspeed disperser (Ultra-Turrax, IKA), followed by cooling at $3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The suspension was degassed before freeze-casting using Teflon molds sitting on a copper plate in contact with dry ice. The cooling rate was estimated at $15\ \mathrm{K}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ followed by freeze-drying.
Different loadings of reactants $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Hmim, and TEA were also investigated following the same procedure as described above. MF-2 was synthesized using Hmim $\cdot924.4~\mathrm{mg}$ , 11.4 mmol), TEA ( $\mathrm{113.9\mg}$ , 1.1 mmol) and zinc nitrate 1 $213.2\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.76 mmol). While, MF-3 was synthesized using Hmim (1.90 g, 23.4 mmol), TEA $(234.7\mathrm{mg}$ , 2.3 mmol) and zinc nitrate ( $439.3~\mathrm{mg}$ , 1.5 mmol). For all cases, TOCNF $(15~\mathrm{g},0.5~\mathrm{wt.}\%)$ and gelatin $(5~\mathrm{g},4~\mathrm{wt}.\%)$ were used. Foams of TOCNF with gelatin (denoted as Ref.) were also prepared as controls without ZIF-L.
## Characterization
Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) was measured using attenuated total reflectance Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) (Varian 610-IR, UK) in the range of 4000–390 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded using a PANalytical X’Pert PRO X-ray system with current, tension, temperature, and step size (2θ) of 40 mA, $45\mathrm{kV}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 0.05, respectively. The apparent densities of the foams were calculated by weighting the samples and measuring their volumes. Nitrogen adsorption was performed using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument and samples were degassed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . The specific surface area were determined using the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) and Langmuir methods. The morphology of the samples was examined using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) conducted on a
JEOL (JSM-7000, Japan) or TEM3000 (Hitachi, Japan) at accelerated voltage of 2 or $1.8\mathrm{kV}$ . Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) measurements were reported using the same equipment with working distance $10\mathrm{mm}$ . Atomic force microscope (AFM) was measured using Nanoscope V, Veeco Instruments (Santa Barbara, CA, USA) in tapping mode. The rheological properties of gelatin-TOCNF gels were measured on a MRC301 rheometer (Anton Paar, Germany) at $20~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ using a sand blasted $40\mathrm{mm}$ parallel plate geometry with a $1\mathrm{mm}$ gap for viscous samples and a $27~\mathrm{mm}$ Couette geometry. A time sweep was measured for 5 minutes, measuring $\mathbf{G}^{\prime}$ and $\mathbf{{G}^{\prime\prime}}$ at $0.1\%$ strain and a frequency of $1\:\mathrm{Hz}$ . For a determination of the yield stress, a shear rate was applied ranging from 0.001 to $10\mathrm{~s~}^{-1}$ and back to 0.001 to measure the shear stress and viscosity response. The Young's modulus was calculated by fitting a line through the elastic region of the Stree-Strain response of the different hybrid foams, and determining the slope of these lines.
## CO2 adsorption studies
$\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption was performed at $0\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C}$ using ASAP 2020 adsorption analyzer. All the samples were evacuated to ultrahigh vacuum at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ before the measurements. The reusability and selectivity of the foams for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture was measured using Thermogravimetric gas sorption analysis performed in a TA Instruments Discovery thermobalance using LABLINE $5.5\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $5.0\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gases and 5-10 mg of samples spread in 100 uL Pt pans. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption was measured by the weight increase upon switching from ${\bf N}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere. After maximum adsorption, the gas was switched back to ${\bf N}_{2}$ for desorption. A flow of 100 mL/min was used for all measurements.
## Results and Discussions
## Materials Characterization
The general synthesis procedure of ZIF-L $@$ TOCNF foams (MF-1, MF-2, and MF-3) is schematic represented in Fig. 1. The TOCNF exhibited a thickness of $5{\cdot}10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and length of $1{-}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S1). The method involves in situ growth of ZIF-L on TOCNF, embed the modified nanofibers in a gelatin matrix, and cool down to induce the change of conformation of gelatin to triple helix structure, followed by freeze‐drying (Fig. 1). The proposed mechanism for the in situ growth modification of the nanofibers is schematically represented in Fig. 2a. The interaction between TOCNF and reactants for each step was followed using FT-IR spectroscopy (Fig. S2) and XRD (Fig. S3). FT-IR spectrum of TOCNF shows peaks at 3300, 1634, and $1055\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to O— H, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , and O—C, respectively (Fig. S2). In the first stage, $Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ ions are coordinated into the functional groups of TOCNF fibers. The hydroxyl and carbonyl groups of TOCNF become broad upon interaction with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions (Fig. 2a). There is no clear change after the addition of TEA to the solution i.e. after formation of $\mathrm{znO}$ crystal that is confirmed using XRD pattern (Fig. S3). ZIF-L is formed after the addition of Hmim. The phase purity of the final product is confirmed using XRD patterns (Fig. 3). Data confirm the formation of leaf-like ZIF (ZIF-L) with high crystallinity (Fig. 3). There is no change in the phase purity using different concentration of zinc nitrate from 0.49 mmol (MF-1) to 1.5 mmol (MF-3) as shown in Fig. 3. The observed peak at $420\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in FT-IR spectra refers to Zn—N (Hmim, Fig. S2). The resultant ZIF-L $@$ TOCNF suspension was then washed to remove any traces of unreacted reagents, and incorporated into a gelatin matrix. ZIF-L $@$ TOCNF interacts via electrostatic interactions with the amine groups of gelatin. The resultant materials were cooled overnight, inducing the physical thermos-reversible gelation which occurs due to the partial recovery of collagen triple-helix structure by disorder-order rearrangement (Fig. 2b).
Divalent zinc ions may cause cross linking of TOCNF and change the fibres rheological properties. The Storage moduli $(\mathbf{G}^{\prime\prime})$ of the gel materials is increased with adding of the reactants $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , TEA, and Hmim (Fig. S4-S5). The addition of zinc salt also increases the ionic strength, and thus reducing the Debye length among the nanofibers, which also contributes to the increase in gel strength. The subsequent addition of TEA and Hmim, lead to the in situ formation of ZIF-L on TOCNF, which has abundant polar groups such as e.g. hydroxyl and carboxyl, offering copious strong binding sites for nucleation, growth and adhesion of ZIF-L crystals. Upon addition of Hmim, the rheological properties of the gel were detrimentally affected, as the ZIF-L particles are believed interpose among the fibers, preventing hydrogen bonding or other interactions between them (Fig. S4-S5). The aforementioned interaction between gelatin and TOCNF was demonstrated by an increase in the Storage modulus and viscosity (Fig. S4-S5). The resultant gels were then directionally frozen to promote ice crystal growth, and the final foam was obtained by freeze‐drying (Fig. 4). The material after freeze-dry were characterized using FT-IR (Fig. S6), SEM (Fig. 5, and Fig. S7), high resolution SEM (Fig. S8), EDX analysis and mapping (Fig. S9), and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption (Fig. 6a). FT-IR spectrum of TOCNF shows characteristic peaks at 3342, 2894, 1604, and $1016\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to bonds of O—H, C—H, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , and C—O, respectively (Fig. S6). The spectrum of Ref. foam (Fig. S6) containing collagen shows peaks corresponding to absorption of $\scriptstyle\nu(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ (amide I, $1,641-1,550\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), $\delta(\mathrm{CH}_{2})$ , and $\delta(\mathrm{CH}_{3})$ $(1,455{-}1,409\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ), $\nu(\mathbf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{N})$ , and $\delta(\mathrm{N-H})$ of amide III $(1,347-1,153\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , and $\nu(\mathbf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{O})$ and $\nu(\mathbf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{O}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{C})$ absorptions of carbohydrate moieties $(1,051-1,024\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ (Belbachir et al., 2009). Foams containing ZIF-L (MF-1, and MF-3) display extra peaks in the region of $500{-}1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1350{-}1500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ referring to the bending and stretching of the imidazolate ring, respectively (Abdelhamid et al., 2017). $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=C}$ and C–N stretches at $1606\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $420\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ refers to Zn—N (imidazole) confirming the presence of ZIF-L frameworks into the formed foams (Fig. S6). Peak of O—H at $3300\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for TOCNF and Ref. becomes broad after formation of ZIF-L due to the interaction and the presence of water molecules in the crystal structure of ZIF-L $\mathrm{(Zn(mim)_{2}{\cdot}(H m i m)_{1/2}{\cdot}(H_{2}O)_{3/2})}$ (Fig. S6). FT-IR spectra confirm the interactions within the foams and support the formation of ZIF-L (Fig. S6).
SEM images of the formed foams confirm the formation of hierarchical porous structure containing mesopore, and macropore structure, which arise due to ice crystal formation. (Fig. 4-5, and Fig. S7-S8). SEM images show white particles refers to ZIFL particles that were confirmed from EDX analysis and mapping (Fig. S9). SEM crosssection image of the synthesized foams was also reported to elucidate the influence of ZIF-L loading over the hierarchical structure of the anisotropic foams, as well as the distribution of them into the gelating-TOCNF networks (Fig. 5). The foams displayed a conventional honeycomb pore structure (Fig. 5). Data show the formation of an anisotropic tubular pore structure as result of the unidirectional freeze-casting process, where the pores run parallel to the freezing direction throughout the material. Highresolution (HR)-SEM images show that ZIF-L particles are embedded into the wall of the foams (Fig. S8). HRSEM images reveals that the particle size of ZIF-L is  100-200 nm (Fig. S8). The presence of TOCNF during the crystal growth leads to a small
particles size. TOCNF enhances the crystal nucleation over growth, and thus decreases the size of ZIF-L crystals (Zhu et al., 2018). On the other hand, it also prevents the aggregation and improves the pore accessibility of ZIF-L particles, and thus may improve the performance of ZIF-L particles for applications including adsorption, catalysis...etc. In sharp contrast to simply blending ZIF-L crystals with cellulose nanocrystals, in situ growth of ZIF-L is simple, controllable, and requires fewer synthesis steps. The percentage of ZIF-L into the foam was determined using EDX analysis. Data shows Zn content of 4.6, 8.5, and $11.0\%$ for MF-1, MF-2, and MF-3, corresponding to ZIF-L of $21\%$ , $39\%$ , and $50\%$ , respectively. The presence of ZIF-L in the final products (MF-1, MF-2, and MF-3) increases with the increase of the reactants amounts. EDX mapping shows also a uniform dispersion of ZIF-L into TOCNF networks (Fig. S9). The resultant foams are ultralight weight as they can rest on top of the stigma of a flower (Fig. 4b). However, they offer excellent mechanical properties and can hold a mass of a $500~\mathrm{g}$ object without any observable damage of the formed foam (Fig. 4c).
The synthesized materials resulted in the formation of extremely porous hybrid foams with large accessible surface area, and with ultralow densities as low as $37.4\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (Table 1), compared to the density of Ref. (without ZIF-L) which is $13.9\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (Table 1). The presence of ZIF-L increases the density of the foams due to the high density of ZIF-L (Table 1), increasing gradually as a function of ZIF-L loading from $19.2\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ for $21\%$ to $22.4\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ and $37.4\mathrm{kg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ for $39\%$ and $50\%$ of ZIF-L, respectively. The porosity of the formed foams was evaluated using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption as shown in Fig. 6a. Data shows BET and Langmuir surface area of $\mathsf{5}{-}14\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 6a, Table 1).
The mechanical properties of the foams were measured by compression testing, which indicates how in situ growth of ZIF-L affect the microstructure of the foams, and the stress-strain curves of the hybrid foams are shown in Fig. S10. Compared to the reference foam containing only gelatin-TOCNF, MF-1 exhibited a significant improvement in the mechanical properties with a Young´s modulus of 2.0 MPa compared to 0.96 MPa of the Ref. foam, which is probably due to an increase in the bulk density of the porous structure of the foams by the presence of small amount of ZIF-L crystals in the cell walls (Chen et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2016). Nevertheless, MF-2 and MF-3 displayed the opposite effect, reducing the Young’s Modulus to 0.68 and 0.24 MPa, respectively; which is explained by the disruption of gelatin-TOCNF network due to the great amount of ZIF-L crystals.
Cellulose is abundant, sustainable, and lightweight polymers with several functional groups. These functional groups may shift the balance of nucleation and growth for synthesizing crystals, and thus decrease the aggregation possibilities of the powder product (Zhu et al., 2018). They also enable sufficient cross-linkers in the resultant foams and off large-strain deformation. Cellulose offers also extrinsic porosities and mechanical flexibility for the resultant ZIF-L foams.
## Applications for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Sorption
Application of the foams for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption was investigated (Fig. 6b-d). Ref. foam comprising TOCNF and gelatin exhibited a significant higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity compared to pure TOCNF, which is attributed to the amine groups present in gelatin. Moreover, the presence of ZIF-L clearly enhances $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of the formed foams (Fig. 6b-d). ZIF-L crystals have one large zero-dimensional pore or cavity with dimensions of $9.4\mathrm{~\AA~~}\times7.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , thus the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption take place via a gate opening mechanism (Chen et al., 2013). The cushion-shaped cavities of ZIF-L ensure higher selectivity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Chen et al., 2013). These cavities offer flexible adsorption compared to the tetrahedral structure of other ZIFs (Chen et al., 2013). Thus, adsorption of all foams containing ZIF-L shows type-I isotherms (Fig. 6b). The capacity of MF-3 is lower than the pure ZIF-L due to the low content of ZIF-L (only $50\%$ of ZIF-L is present in MF-3). Thus, MF-3 shows adsorption of $0.75\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ compared to pure ZIF-L that shows adsorption capacity of $0.90\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , that matches previous reported value (Chen et al., 2013). ZIF-L with low loading offers good $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity.
The reusability of the hybrid foams as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbent was tested by carrying out multiple cycles of adsorption-desorption at room temperature (Fig. 7a). Reusability of adsorbents is of sum importance considering that for practical application; an adsorbent should not only exhibit high adsorption capacity and high selectivity but also almost provide long-term stability over the performance in multiple cycles (Fig. 7b). There is insignificant decrease in the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ weight-gain over repeated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ cycles, suggesting the excellent reusability of the foams as adsorbent. However, the slightly lower weights gain after the first cycle could be attributed to the fact that a part of the adsorption sites are already occupied by ${\bf N}_{2}$ when the gas is switched from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ apparent selectivity of the hybrid foams, considering the ratio of gained
weight $\%$ upon $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, multiplied by the density ratio of both gases (ρN2/ρCO2), and results are presented in Fig. 7b. The results demonstrate the high selectivity of the foams due to characteristic adsorption properties of ZIF-L attributed to the cushion-shaped cavities that ideally accommodate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Thus, the selectivity significantly increases with the increase of ZIF-L content (Fig. 7b). MF-3 exhibits almost 7 times higher selectivity than MF-1.
ZIFs are promising adsorbents for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. They showed reasonable high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity preferentially over nitrogen and other gases (Chen et al., 2013). Our materials showed high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity compared to pure MOFs materials such as ZIF-8 (Nune et al., 2010), ZIF-25, ZIF-71, ZIF-93, ZIF-96, ZIF-97 (Morris et al., 2010) (Table 2). To the best of our knowledge, few types of hierarchically porous materials containing MOFs have been reported for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Graphene-ZIF-8 hybrid aerogel (GZAn) materials were successfully prepared by a two-step reduction strategy and a layer-by-layer assembly method (Jiang et al., 2018). The materials 1 $\mathrm{\mathbf{MOF\%}}$ is $88.6\%$ ) showed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $0.99{\mathrm{~mmol}}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Jiang et al., 2018). Foam consisting of MOFs (such as HKUST-1 and ZIF-8) and
mesoporous silica was synthesized using a layer-by-layer (LBL) method by 10 cycles of HKUST-1 thin film growth (Shekhah et al., 2012). The foam was applied for high pressure $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption  (Shekhah et al., 2012). Results showed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of ${\sim}0.80\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ (Shekhah et al., 2012). The materials consisting of TOCNF-ZIF-L offers several advantages compared to the powder ZIF-L. The adsorption capacity of $50\%$ ZIF- $\mathbf{\cdotL}$ ( $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity is $0.75\mathrm{mmol\cdotg^{-1}}.$ ) in the foam is closely comparative with the pure powder ZIF-L materials ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity is $0.90\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}},$ ). TOCNF and Ref. are relative weak $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbent with an uptake capacity of 0.11 and $0.35\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar (Fig. 6d). The combination of TOCNF and ZIF-L improves $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of the hybrid foam via synergistic effect. The hierarchical porous structure of ZIF-L@TOCNF can facilitate the strong interaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with TOCNF and ZIF-L in the micropores. Furthermore, it offers fast mass transfer of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ into the adsorbent through mesopores and macropores. The presence of hydrophobic materials such as ZIFs may decrease the hydrophilic nature of cellulose.
## Conclusions
A new and straightforward synthetic strategy was developed to prepare leaf-like zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-L)-cellulose hybrid foam at room temperature using water as solvent. The method procedure is simple, fast, and environmentally friendly. This method provides effective strategy for developing multi-functional foam. The produced foams are ultralight weight with good mechanical properties. They have good capacity for selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption with low loading of ZIF-L $(21-50\%$ ). The hybrids foams are promising for several applications such as adsorption, separation, and catalysis.
